 "notes": "The controller is a state machine that grasps from the top and plans with inverse kinematics (IK). Final version: v24.\n\nPractice results: v14 scored 39/40 on seeds 100000\u2013100039 (v24 releases the same way, plus 0.5 s of waiting and an untested retreat safeguard; it was not re-run on that set). On a harder mixed set of 40 seeds, which included 7 seeds that had failed before, v24 scored 35/40: 4 timeouts and 1 drop.\n\nHow it works:\n- **Hand orientation:** the palm faces down and the fingers point along the hand's yaw. The cube is pinched between the thumb and the curled index and middle fingers, along the grasp site's x axis.\n- **Grasp planning:** it tries the 4 hand yaws that line up with the target cube's faces. For each, it solves IK (damped least squares with a posture pull toward home, using the model from `robot.model`) for the grasp pose and for a pose 14.5 cm above it. It retries from random starting poses, rejects bad solutions with a collision check (arm vs torso, table and other cubes, open hand) and picks the lowest-cost one.\n- **Moves:** it goes to the pose above the cube by joint-space interpolation (cosine profile). It then descends in a straight vertical line toward the measured cube position, closes the hand over 0.7 s and lifts to z \u2248 0.95.\n- **Grasp check:** it checks that the cube came up with the hand and records the cube's offset and yaw relative to the hand.\n- **Placing:** it plans the place pose with the same 4-yaw search on the destination cube, correcting for the recorded offset. It lowers slowly while correcting sideways from the measured positions. It stops when the cube sits within 3 mm of resting height and has stopped moving.\n- **Releasing:** it removes downward pressure, opens the thumb (closure 0.5), waits, then uncurls the fingers slowly and lifts away slowly. If the cube rises with the hand, it moves back down and shifts sideways, up to 3 times.\n- **Retries:** if the grasp is lost or the stack isn't confirmed after the retreat, it starts again from grasp planning.\n\nKnown weaknesses:\n- **Release can still knock the cube off.** Uncurling the fingers or retreating while the thumb still touches the cube sometimes pushes the top cube off. The retry usually recovers but costs about 10 s, so some episodes time out.\n- **A release variant I abandoned.** Moving the hand 1 cm forward before opening helped in an offline test. In full runs it caused drops when the destination was close to the body, so v24 doesn't use it.\n- **Regrasping a pushed cube is unreliable.** A cube that was pushed far away (x > 0.3) is hard to regrasp.\n- **One unexplained sweep.** In seed 100075, two cubes were knocked off the table (the cause wasn't diagnosed; I suspect a joint-space move passing through them).\n- **Untested conditions.** I haven't tested different physics or sensor bias beyond what the practice seeds include.",
